Subject: [#94-12-29] HUMOR: Goliath syndrome (was Pentium jokes)

At 9:24 PM 11/30/94, Nick Szabo wrote:
>Why is everybody beating up on Intel [...?]
>Perhaps it's because folks are jealous of their dominant position
>in the market with what some aesthetes feel is an ugly architecture?

<begin conjecture mode>
It's so commonplace for humans to pick on the big guy (in whatever context)
that I suspect there's probably an evolutionary reason.  Sniping at and
harassing (or deposing and killing) an alpha male probably has reproductive
survival value by giving the various betas a chance to pollinate the poseys
with their own heredity.  There's a whole arms race there between the
dominance-submission complex and the
kill-everybody-else-so-I'm-the-only-male strategy; I suspect that when a
sufficiently dangerous male of the latter type appears to have arisen, the
submission reflexes are overridden and it becomes more advantageous to
topple Goliath.  This might explain why everybody seems to love an underdog
and it's so much _fun_ to see dominant types get their comeuppance.
<end conjecture mode>

