> >
> >Dave,
> >You've been talking to me a lot about this. Whant to handle the follow up
> >on this one?
> 
> What?  I was the first poster Phil's talking about.  How do you create
> value just by printing money?  Money itself is useful as a medium of
> exchange (and hence loans bear interest), but increasing the money supply
> in excess of the overall growth of the economy _does_ cause inflation.
> Those who create it (the feds, or the Fed) get to spend it; you don't.

   I think this is what Geoff meant. Increasing money without
increasing the overall value of the economy causes inflation. Imagine
a society that only had 1 dollar circulating forever. As value
increased and the standard of living went up, that dollar would
come into increasing demand and continually be subdivided. This would
actually cause deflation and sooner of later a 1 penny would buy
a Rolls Royce. (at the same time, people's wages would have to decline)
However, if the money supply is increased as the economy grows, inflation is 
controlled. 

    And actually, when the fed buys bonds (increases the money
supply), we get to spend it. That's how their "economic stimulus"
works. They dump money on the markets, people get that money,
their income goes up, they consume more, higher consumption produces
higher demand, higher demand can lead to higher employment, and
as the higher demand causes prices to rise.

    Not to mention that what the Fed defines as the money supply
differs from the notion many ordinary people have. The Fed can
"increase the money supply" merely by lowering the reserve
requirement for banks. According to the Fed, credit card companies
can increase the money supply on their own. If Extropy issued an
Extropian Credit Card, they would be adding to the money supply.

     Printing money can definately raise the inflation rate. However,
it's not the only thing that can do it and sometimes inflation
is a good thing. (if you're a debter)



