From: "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net>
Date: Thu, 1 Dec 1994 12:36:29 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-41] BASICS: 'Nother Quigley Quote

 goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU (Phil Goetz) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-11-547:
>Minsky:
>>Morals, schmorals.  In any society -- and humans cannot work without one
>>because of their neoteny--each person represents a large investment.
>>Surely the "market" discourages killing.  Maybe it's simply economics, not
>>ethics!
>>
>>When there's unemployment or other kinds of labor surplus, then, this would
>>predict that the killing rate would rise.

 An unemployed person will spend less on self-defense, but most forms of
protection need fairly long-term planning (you don't forget karate overnight,
and you probably wouldn't sell your gun just because you lost your job), so
I don't expect this effect to be noticeable.

>This is another "tragedy of the commons".  A person is a communal resource.
>Your value to society may be large, but your value to Joe Crackhead is
>not as high as the value to him of the contents of your wallet.

 My value to myself is fairly high in comparison to my value to "society",
so the amount I spend on self-defense isn't too far below the economically
optimal level. There is some tragedy of the commons effect though; Intel
has no practical way to express the fact that it would value more babies
(as future customers), so production of babies may be dropping below
optimal levels in developed countries.

>What you have to do, of course, is privatize yourself.
>Sell part ownership of yourself to all the crackheads. ;)

 This prompts me to pass on a quote from economist Robert Shiller:
     Sell yourself short!
 He is trying to establish for claims on future income for groups of
people, to allow people to partially hedge the risks associated with
their own income.
